    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-70292",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5}),$ which of the following shows the curve $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "In order to graph the function, it's helpful to know the period. In order to determine the period, we first need to determine the scaling factor.\n\nNotice that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})$ can be written as $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5}x).$ So, it takes the form $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)$ with $B=\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nThe horizontal scaling factor is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{B}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{5})}=5.\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\n\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{B}=\\pi \\cdot 5=5\\pi .\n$$\n\nNow that we know the period is $5\\pi ,$ we can draw the general shape of the tangent function, but stretch it so that it fills up the interval $(-\\frac{5\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2}).$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14254788/assets/ec77ab8cf1ed3d66c0715abbe4f803d41cb876363307bc3f4e27394c0161053d.png)",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
